course: dictionary
lesson: 13481
title: Identically
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Identically means in exactly the same way, with no differences at all. It is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done. Look at the root word: identical means exactly alike, and the ending -ly turns it into a word for how.
explain: «The twins were dressed identically, in red hats and blue coats.» Nobody could tell which twin was which, because nothing was different. Both twins wore exactly the same clothes in exactly the same way.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «The twins were dressed in exactly the same way.» It still makes sense, so you have got it. Identically is often used in maths, in science and in everyday talk.
fact: Identically means in exactly the same way, with no differences.
fact: «Identically» is an adverb, because it tells you how something is done.
fact: Two shapes that are identically sized are congruent: they match exactly in size.
fact: In a science test, every part of the experiment must be done identically, or the test is not fair.
fact: The phrase «identically dressed» means wearing exactly the same clothes.
exercise: «The two robots were built identically, with the same parts in the same places.» What does identically mean here? | With lots of different colours | In a hurry | Very slowly | *In exactly the same way
exercise: Which two shapes are identically sized? | *Two squares that are exactly the same size | A square and a circle | One big triangle and one small triangle | A rectangle with four sides
exercise: Find and copy the adverb in this sentence: «The twins walked identically down the stairs.» | stairs | *identically | down | walked
test: «The two plants were grown identically, with the same water and the same sunlight.» Why is this fair for a science test? | *Because both plants were treated in exactly the same way | Because one plant was bigger at the start | Because they were grown in different pots | Because one plant had more water
test: What does identically mean? | Very loudly and quickly | *In exactly the same way, with no differences | Only just, a little bit | At the front of the line
exam: Which sentence uses identically correctly? | The bus stop is identically tall | I felt identically happy yesterday | *Both teams wore identically coloured shirts, so nobody could tell them apart | The sun rose identically in a hurry
